 62   c  ABST$Experimental nuclear spectroscopic data are evaluated for 12      
 62  2c  known nuclides of mass number A=62 (Sc, Ti, V, Cr, Mn, Fe, Co, Ni, Cu, 
 62  3c  Zn, Ga, Ge). Detailed evaluated information are presented for each     
 62  4c  reaction and decay. Recommended values combining all available data    
 62  5c  are provided for all the spectroscopic properties of each level,       
 62  6c  |g-ray, and decay radiation. Following is the status of structure and  
 62  7c  decay information available for each of the nuclides. {B{+62}Sc}: only 
 62  8c  the isotopic identification has been made, with unknown half-life of   
 62  9c  its g.s., and its decay scheme. {B{+62}Ti}: half-life of the g.s. is   
 62  Ac  unknown, but the first (2+) and (4+) states have been reported, the    
 62  Bc  second at 1506 keV.  {B{+62}V}: information is available for only the  
 62  Cc  ground state, its decay scheme is poorly known, with three |g rays,    
 62  Dc  and one level in {+62}Cr. {B{+62}Cr}: only three excited states are    
 62  Ec  known up to 1458 keV, with half-life of one excited state. Its decay   
 62  Fc  scheme is poorly known, with five |g rays and three levels in the      
 62  Fc  daughter nucleus.  {B{+62}Mn}: two beta-decaying activities are known, 
 62  Gc  but with poorly known decay schemes. Ten excited states are known up   
 62  Hc  to 1531 keV, and up to J=(8), but with no half-life known for any of   
 62  Ic  the excited states.  {B{+62}Fe}: 23 excited states are known up to     
 62  Jc  5475 keV, and up to 10+, with the half-lives known for only the first  
 62  Kc  2+ and 4+ states. Its decay scheme is poorly known with only one |g    
 62  Lc  ray and one level in {+62}Co. {B{+62}Co}: two beta-decaying activities 
 62  Mc  are known, with their decay decay schemes fairly well established.     
 62  Nc  Large number of levels are up to 6950 keV, and up to (13+), but        
 62  Oc  half-lives of only two excited states are known. {B{+62}Ni}: stable    
 62  Pc  nucleus with a large number of levels known up to 24.5 MeV excitation, 
 62  Qc  and up to (24-). Half-lives of 30 excited states are known.            
 62  Rc  {B{+62}Cu}: a large number of levels are known up to 10.9 MeV          
 62  Sc  excitation, and up to (14+), but half-lives known for only seven       
 62  Tc  excited states. Its decay scheme is fairly well established.           
 62  Uc  {B{+62}Zn}: a rare nucleus with co-existence of many different types   
 62  Vc  of structures: five superdeformed bands, 11 well-deformed bands, two   
 62  Vc  terminating bands, and nine normal-deformed bands, up to an excitation 
 62  Wc  energy of 42.5 MeV, and up to (35-), with half-lives known for 18      
 62  Xc  excited states. Its decay scheme is well established. {B{+62}Ga}: many 
 62  Yc  levels are known up to 16.8 MeV excitation, and up to (21+), but       
 62  Zc  half-life of only one excited state is known. Its decay scheme is of   
 62  ac  special interest due to superallowed decay to {+62}Zn, and has been    
 62  bc  intensively pursued in recent works.  {B{+62}Ge}: only two possible    
 62  cc  excited states have been suggested up to 2285 keV, with no knowledge   
 62  dc  of their J|p values. Its decay scheme to {+62}Ga is fairly well known, 
 62  ec  with probable involvement of a superallowed decay. {B{+62}As}: this    
 62  fc  nuclide has not been investigated, it is included only for             
 62  gc  completeness. {B{+62}Se}: experimental search for this nuclide by      
 62  hc  2016Bl05 did not yield any events, implying that this nucleus is       
 62  ic  likely unbound towards two-proton emission as theoretical S(2p)=-3290  
 62  jc  keV (2019Mo01). This work supersedes data in earlier ENSDF evaluation  
 62  xc  of A=62 nuclei by 2012Ni08.                                            
 62   c  CUT$Literature available up to June 30, 2023 has been consulted, with  
 62  2c  the help of the primary bibliographic source, the NSR database         
 62  3c  (2011Pr03) available at Brookhaven National Laboratory web page:       
 62  #c  www.nndc.bnl.gov/nsr/                                                  
 62   c  COMM$The statistical analysis of |g-ray data and deduced level schemes 
 62  2c  is carried out through a suite of current versions of various computer 
 62  3c  codes available at the IAEA-Nuclear Data Sheets website:               
 62  4c  https://www-nds.iaea.org/public/ensdf_pgm/index.htm. The direct        
 62  5c  feedings to excited states in |b{+-} and |e decays have generally been 
 62  6c  computed from I(|g+ce) intensity balances at each level. The           
 62  7c  BetaShape-v2.3 code (2019Mo35) was used to deduce log| {ft} values and 
 62  8c  average |b energies in |b{+-} and |e decay datasets. Theoretical total 
 62  9c  conversion coefficients were calculated using the BrIcc-v2.3e code     
 62  Ac  (2008Ki07) for frozen-orbit option. In cases where weighted averaging  
 62  Bc  procedures have been used, the assigned uncertainty in the result is   
 62  Cc  generally not lower than the lowest uncertainty amongst the data       
 62  Dc  points used in the process.                                            
 62   c  ACKN$Earlier evaluations (2012Ni08, 2000Hu18, 1999Si11, 1990Ki08,      
 62  2c  1979Ha01, 1974Ve13), and compilations of recent articles in the XUNDL  
 62  3c  database provided valuable resources for the present work. The         
 62  4c  evaluators are grateful to reviewers of this work at different stages  
 62  5c  of this evaluation, and to the personnel at the NNDC, Brookhaven       
 62   c  PERM$B. Singh: balraj@mcmaster.ca.  X. Huang: huang@ciae.ac.cn FUND$   
 62  2c  B. Singh's work was supported through a contract from Brookhaven       
 62  3c  National Laboratory, Upton, N.Y., USA. Work of X. Huang and X. Wang    
 62  4c  was supported by the China Institute of Atomic Energy, Beijing, China. 

